1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:02,990
Imagine an AI search answer with three

2
00:00:03,010 --> 00:00:06,133
citations. You check every reference.

3
00:00:06,309 --> 00:00:08,208
All three papers exist.

4
00:00:08,370 --> 00:00:09,948
Can you now trust the answer?

5
00:00:10,110 --> 00:00:11,030
Hold that thought.

6
00:00:11,193 --> 00:00:13,479
What exactly did your check establish?

7
00:00:16,401 --> 00:00:18,018
It established that the references are

8
00:00:18,038 --> 00:00:19,911
real. That is useful.

9
00:00:20,087 --> 00:00:22,012
But it did not establish that the answer

10
00:00:22,032 --> 00:00:24,474
represents them faithfully, or that the

11
00:00:24,494 --> 00:00:27,025
system found the evidence needed to answer

12
00:00:27,045 --> 00:00:27,875
your question.

13
00:00:28,798 --> 00:00:31,242
A fabricated citation is one kind of

14
00:00:31,262 --> 00:00:34,167
failure. A reference that does not exist

15
00:00:34,187 --> 00:00:36,177
can be exposed by checking it.

16
00:00:36,352 --> 00:00:38,523
But passing that check answers only the

17
00:00:38,543 --> 00:00:39,850
existence question.

18
00:00:40,412 --> 00:00:42,417
Our imaginary answer passes.

19
00:00:42,580 --> 00:00:44,905
The three references are real within the

20
00:00:44,925 --> 00:00:47,031
illustration. We still have two more

21
00:00:47,051 --> 00:00:48,770
questions to investigate.

22
00:00:49,672 --> 00:00:52,349
A real source can still be misrepresented.

23
00:00:52,498 --> 00:00:55,661
In this invented example, a paper reports

24
00:00:55,681 --> 00:00:58,213
an uncertain result, while the answer

25
00:00:58,233 --> 00:01:00,532
describes it as a settled conclusion.

26
00:01:00,694 --> 00:01:02,929
The reference exists; the description is

27
00:01:02,949 --> 00:01:03,612
wrong.

28
00:01:04,189 --> 00:01:06,206
Checking the writing against the source

29
00:01:06,226 --> 00:01:07,636
tackles that problem.

30
00:01:07,785 --> 00:01:10,562
Now suppose we correct the wording and

31
00:01:10,582 --> 00:01:12,778
every sentence fairly represents the

32
00:01:12,798 --> 00:01:14,169
papers it cites.

33
00:01:14,332 --> 00:01:16,334
Is our investigation finished?

34
00:01:17,234 --> 00:01:19,670
Here is the harder failure to see.

35
00:01:19,833 --> 00:01:22,506
Another relevant paper never reaches the

36
00:01:22,526 --> 00:01:23,845
answer-writing step.

37
00:01:24,021 --> 00:01:25,856
The answer cannot show you evidence it

38
00:01:25,876 --> 00:01:27,054
never received.

39
00:01:27,229 --> 00:01:29,671
It can be fluent, carefully worded and

40
00:01:29,691 --> 00:01:32,094
neatly cited, yet still rest on an

41
00:01:32,114 --> 00:01:33,485
inadequate selection.

42
00:01:34,044 --> 00:01:36,506
The preface asks us to examine this

43
00:01:36,526 --> 00:01:38,503
retrieval half of the system.

44
00:01:38,755 --> 00:01:41,235
Faithful writing and adequate retrieval

45
00:01:41,255 --> 00:01:42,781
are separate questions.

46
00:01:42,931 --> 00:01:45,706
A citation list alone settles neither.

47
00:01:46,603 --> 00:01:48,000
Try a thought experiment.

48
00:01:48,150 --> 00:01:50,320
Give two writers different envelopes of

49
00:01:50,340 --> 00:01:52,304
papers about the same question.

50
00:01:52,480 --> 00:01:55,436
Both writers describe every supplied paper

51
00:01:55,456 --> 00:01:59,045
perfectly. One envelope omits an important

52
00:01:59,065 --> 00:02:00,345
piece of evidence.

53
00:02:00,909 --> 00:02:03,450
Excellent writing cannot put that missing

54
00:02:03,470 --> 00:02:05,124
paper back into the envelope.

55
00:02:05,287 --> 00:02:08,050
The illustration does not prove how any

56
00:02:08,070 --> 00:02:09,867
real product behaves.

57
00:02:10,029 --> 00:02:12,535
It shows why inspecting the final prose

58
00:02:12,555 --> 00:02:14,738
and inspecting evidence selection do

59
00:02:14,758 --> 00:02:15,949
different jobs.

60
00:02:16,842 --> 00:02:19,172
If a paper is missing from an answer,

61
00:02:19,206 --> 00:02:20,770
start asking questions.

62
00:02:20,932 --> 00:02:22,600
Was it in the searchable collection?

63
00:02:22,749 --> 00:02:24,726
Did it enter a candidate set?

64
00:02:24,889 --> 00:02:25,809
How was it ranked?

65
00:02:25,971 --> 00:02:27,690
What evidence was passed onward?

66
00:02:27,865 --> 00:02:29,211
What did the writer use?

67
00:02:29,789 --> 00:02:32,270
Those are diagnostic possibilities, not a

68
00:02:32,290 --> 00:02:34,216
verdict about where a real failure

69
00:02:34,236 --> 00:02:36,549
occurred. The goal is to locate the

70
00:02:36,569 --> 00:02:39,294
decision, using what the system documents

71
00:02:39,314 --> 00:02:40,904
and what you can actually inspect.

72
00:02:41,805 --> 00:02:45,194
Libraries license, configure, evaluate and

73
00:02:45,214 --> 00:02:47,823
pay for the retrieval side of these tools.

74
00:02:47,972 --> 00:02:49,898
Tay argues that someone in the library

75
00:02:49,918 --> 00:02:52,140
needs enough understanding to compare

76
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:54,731
systems and ask which layer a problem

77
00:02:54,751 --> 00:02:55,761
belongs to.

78
00:02:56,328 --> 00:02:57,831
You do not need to become a retrieval

79
00:02:57,851 --> 00:03:00,473
engineer. You need to keep three questions

80
00:03:00,493 --> 00:03:03,308
separate: is the source real, is it

81
00:03:03,328 --> 00:03:05,460
represented faithfully, and was the

82
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:06,993
evidence selection adequate?

83
00:03:07,168 --> 00:03:08,797
The papers you never see deserve a

84
00:03:08,817 --> 00:03:09,828
question too.
